//        for i in [0,k)   y[i] = z[i]
//    This can be done in-place and in a single pass by updating
//    pairs (i, j = k-1-i) simultaneously, because both new values
//    depend only on the *old* y[i] and y[j]. For odd k we handle
//    the center element separately. This reduces:
//        - memory footprint (no z array)
//        - memory traffic (one pass instead of two)
//        - number of noarr traversals per k (2 instead of 3).
//
// 2. Slightly simplify the k-loop to use for_each directly on a
//    1D 'k' structure. All iteration is still expressed via
//    noarr::traverser and state objects, as required.
//
[[gnu::flatten, gnu::noinline]]
void kernel_durbin(auto r, auto y) {
	using namespace noarr;

